; beatrax.app — Cloudflare DNS zone
;
; Import: Cloudflare dashboard → beatrax.app → DNS → Records
;         → Import and Export → Import DNS records → upload this file.
;
; Set every A, AAAA and CNAME record below to DNS only (grey cloud) until
; GitHub has issued the certificate for beatrax.app. Proxying before then
; hides the origin from GitHub's validation and the certificate never issues.
; Once "Enforce HTTPS" is available under the repository's Pages settings,
; proxying can be turned on if you want Cloudflare in front of it.
;
; .app is on the HSTS preload list, so browsers refuse plain HTTP for this
; domain by policy. That is the intended behaviour and needs no record.

$ORIGIN beatrax.app.
$TTL 3600

; ── Apex → GitHub Pages ───────────────────────────────────────────────
; The four published Pages anycast addresses. All four belong here; GitHub
; balances across them, so dropping one costs availability for no gain.
@       3600  IN  A       185.199.108.153
@       3600  IN  A       185.199.109.153
@       3600  IN  A       185.199.110.153
@       3600  IN  A       185.199.111.153
@       3600  IN  AAAA    2606:50c0:8000::153
@       3600  IN  AAAA    2606:50c0:8001::153
@       3600  IN  AAAA    2606:50c0:8002::153
@       3600  IN  AAAA    2606:50c0:8003::153

; ── www → the same site ───────────────────────────────────────────────
; GitHub redirects www to the apex on its own, because the repository's
; CNAME file names the apex. No redirect rule is needed here.
www     3600  IN  CNAME   beatrax-app.github.io.

; ── GitHub organisation verification ──────────────────────────────────
; Proves beatrax-app owns this domain. That is what lets Pages sites in the
; organisation serve it, and what stops anyone else from claiming it.
_github-challenge-beatrax-app-org  3600  IN  TXT  "0b5a763863f5ae4b2231a4dc9d2549"

; ── Certificate authority authorisation ───────────────────────────────
; Pages certificates come from Let's Encrypt. digicert and pki.goog are the
; authorities Cloudflare issues its own edge certificates from, so they stay
; authorised in case these records are proxied later.
@       3600  IN  CAA     0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
@       3600  IN  CAA     0 issue "digicert.com"
@       3600  IN  CAA     0 issue "pki.goog"
@       3600  IN  CAA     0 issuewild ";"

; ── Mail: this domain sends none ──────────────────────────────────────
; A domain with no mail policy is a free spoofing target. These say, in the
; three places a receiver looks, that nothing legitimate is ever sent from
; beatrax.app.
@       3600  IN  MX      0 .
@       3600  IN  TXT     "v=spf1 -all"
_dmarc  3600  IN  TXT     "v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:info@nightworks.io; aspf=s; adkim=s"
*._domainkey  3600  IN  TXT  "v=DKIM1; p="
